{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_44","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'python3 /workspace/tools.py read paper:dmr --start-line 1 --lines 9'","aggregated_output":"/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n{\n  \"id\": \"paper:dmr\",\n  \"url\": \"https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AAAI/article/view/5346\",\n  \"version\": \"content-sha256:656c9e5eec79c9843e907a555ede2bf0a66a0db3515916af3d655080057c5a75\",\n  \"access_scope\": \"author README only, not the blocked publisher paper\",\n  \"text_sha256\": \"656c9e5eec79c9843e907a555ede2bf0a66a0db3515916af3d655080057c5a75\",\n  \"total_lines\": 9,\n  \"start_line\": 1,\n  \"next_line\": 10,\n  \"truncated\": false,\n  \"text\": \"1: # DMR\\n2: Deep Match to Rank Model for Personalized Click-Through Rate Prediction\\n3: \\n4: This code for DMR is implemented based on the Alimama Dataset (https://tianchi.aliyun.com/dataset/dataDetail?dataId=56), references the codes of DIEN (https://github.com/mouna99/dien).\\n5: \\n6: \\\"alimama_sampled.txt\\\" is a sampled dataset for testing the code.\\n7: \\n8: Notice that, the user behavior data in this dataset contains only category, brand and behavior type, but no item.\\n9: \"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_45","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python3 /workspace/tools.py read paper:din --find 'sum of' --lines 24\"","aggregated_output":"/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n/bin/bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale (C.UTF-8)\n{\n  \"id\": \"paper:din\",\n  \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.06978v4\",\n  \"version\": \"content-sha256:5fee25f33f375e51b3ee9417d376ff9dbe2ea3628c932a7679ddfeb0feaaade2\",\n  \"access_scope\": \"archived exact-version primary text; caching does not establish a local mechanism result\",\n  \"text_sha256\": \"83fb9eeca31e2fa3c557b6f08b893ad0018cefb33da6e00952136b9ce17e607e\",\n  \"total_lines\": 831,\n  \"start_line\": 120,\n  \"next_line\": 144,\n  \"truncated\": false,\n  \"text\": \"120: Attention mechanism originates from Neural Machine Translation (NMT) field (Bahdanau\\n121: et al., 2015).\\n122: NMT takes a weighted sum of all the annotations to get an expected annotation and focuses only on information relevant to the generation of next target word.\\n123: A recent work, DeepIntent (Zhai\\n124: et al., 2016) applies attention in the context of search advertising. Similar to NMT, they use RNN(Williams and\\n125: Zipser, 1989) to model text, then learn one global hidden vector to help paying attention on the key words in each query. It is shown that the use of attention can help capturing the main intent of query or ad.\\n126: DIN designs a local activation unit to soft-search for relevant user behaviors and takes a weighted sum pooling to obtain the adaptive representation of user interests with respect to a given ad. The user representation vector varies over different ads, which is different from DeepIntent in which there is no interaction between ad and user.\\n127: We make code publicly available, and further show how to successfully deploy DIN in one of the world’s largest advertising systems with novel developed techniques for training large scale deep networks with hundreds of millions of parameters.\\n128: 3. Background\\n129: In e-commerce sites, such as Alibaba, advertisements are natural goods. In the rest of this paper, without special declaration, we regard ads as goods. Figure 1 briefly illustrates the running procedure of display advertising system in Alibaba, which consists of two main stages: i) matching stage which generates list of candidate ads relevant to the visiting user via methods like collaborative filtering, ii) ranking stage which predicts CTR for each given ad and then selects top ranked ones.\\n130: Everyday, hundreds of millions of users visit the e-commerce site, leaving us with lots of user behavior data which contributes critically in building matching and ranking models.\\n131: It is worth mentioning that users with rich historical behaviors contain diverse interests.\\n132: For example, a young mother has browsed goods including woolen coat, T-shits, earrings, tote bag, leather handbag and children’s coat recently. These behavior data give us hints about her shopping interests.\\n133: When she visits the e-commerce site, system displays a suitable ad to her, for example a new handbag.\\n134: Obviously the displayed ad only matches or activates part of interests of this mother.\\n135: In summary, interests of user with rich behaviors are diverse and could be locally activated given certain ads. We show later in this paper making use of these characteristics plays important role for building CTR prediction model.\\n136: Figure 1. Illustration of running procedure of display advertising system in Alibaba, in which user behavior data plays important roles.\\n137: 4. Deep Interest Network\\n138: Different from sponsored search, users come into display advertising system without explicitly expressed intentions.\\n139: Effective approaches are required to extract user interests from rich historical behaviors when building the CTR prediction model.\\n140: Features that depict users and ads are the basic elements in the CTR modeling of advertisement system.\\n141: Making use of these features reasonably and mining information from them are critical.\\n142: 4.1. Feature Representation\\n143: Data in industrial CTR prediction tasks is mostly in a multi-group categorial form, for example, [weekday=Friday, gender=Female,\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
